{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["For the name El befits all greatness; it is exceedingly exalted above all praise.<br>To Abraham my son, may his light shine, son of Meir, may his rock preserve him [n: עוז read as יצ\"ו = ישמרו צורו ויעזרהו, resolved by inference from the formulaic pattern], who undertook to expound the Scroll.<br>Uttered by Abraham the Sephardi, called Ibn Ezra: there is no help but from HaShem, who decrees an eternal decree upon the heart of the discerning when he awakes — and in a dream He speaks within him — and upon Him he leans when he begins to do every deed, and He reminds him continually before the going forth of his lips.<br>And behold, there is in this Scroll no mention of HaShem, yet it is among the Sacred Writings. And many answered that it is from 'the Place.' This is not correct, for HaShem is never called 'Place' in all the Sacred Writings; rather He is called Ma'on, which is eternally exalted. And our ancients, of blessed memory, called Him 'Place' because every place is full of His glory. Furthermore, what reason is there for the word 'from'? But what seems correct to me is that the composer of this Scroll is Mordecai, and this is the meaning of 'and he sent letters' — and all of them are copies of one book, which is the Scroll, like the meaning of פתשגן (a copy). And the Persians translated it, and it was written in the chronicles of their kings. And they were idolaters, and they would write in place of the honored and awesome Name the name of their abomination, as the Cutheans did, who wrote in place of בראשית ברא אלהים — ברא אשימא (they created Ashima). And so it is to the glory of HaShem that Mordecai did not mention Him in the Scroll." "<b>And it came to pass in the days.</b> This Ahasuerus was after Cyrus and Zerubbabel with the exile, in the Second Temple — only the Temple was not yet built. And in my opinion he is Artaxerxes; and this is the meaning of 'and in the kingdom of Ahasuerus, at the beginning of his reign,' as I shall expound in the book of Ezra. And the meaning of 'and it came to pass in the days' concerns all these things that are recounted in this Scroll. And the meaning 'he is Ahasuerus' is not like 'Abram — he is Abraham.' It is possible that among the ancient kings of Persia there was a king whose name was Ahasuerus, and both of them were kings over Persia and Media — only this second Ahasuerus ruled over other provinces, which are between India and Cush, for the land of Media and Persia is north of the land of Israel, while India is only east and Cush south."], :book :esther, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The opening lines (לשם אל…לפרש המגילה) are a dedicatory preface; עוז at the end of the second line appears to be an abbreviation or fragment, resolved here by inference from the adjacent יצ\"ו formula."}, :source-witnesses [["Kol Sason, Krotoschin, 1840" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001728315"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:29:49.737643229Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}